## Test Strategy Components

- **Load Testing**: Simulate realistic user traffic patterns with gradual ramp-up
- **Rate Limiting**: Implement request throttling and batching to respect API limits
- **Error Handling**: Test retry mechanisms with exponential backoff and circuit breakers
- **Contract Testing**: Validate API responses against schemas and documentation
- **Monitoring**: Track performance metrics, error rates, and system resource utilization

## Tools & Implementation

```javascript
// Example retry logic with exponential backoff
const retryRequest = async (url, retries = 3) => {
  for (let i = 0; i < retries; i++) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      if (response.ok) return response;

      // Exponential backoff: 1s, 2s, 4s
      const delay = Math.pow(2, i) * 1000;
      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay));
    } catch (error) {
      if (i === retries - 1) throw error;
    }
  }
};

// Rate limiting implementation
class RateLimiter {
  constructor(maxRequests = 100, timeWindow = 60000) {
    this.maxRequests = maxRequests;
    this.timeWindow = timeWindow;
    this.requests = [];
  }

  async makeRequest(requestFn) {
    const now = Date.now();
    this.requests = this.requests.filter(time => now - time < this.timeWindow);

    if (this.requests.length >= this.maxRequests) {
      const waitTime = this.timeWindow - (now - this.requests[0]);
      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, waitTime));
    }

    this.requests.push(now);
    return await requestFn();
  }
}
```

## Testing Phases

1. **Baseline Testing**: Establish performance metrics under normal load
2. **Stress Testing**: Identify breaking points and failure modes
3. **Spike Testing**: Test sudden traffic increases and recovery
4. **Endurance Testing**: Validate performance over extended periods
5. **Rate Limit Testing**: Verify behavior at and beyond rate limits

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for high-throughput ingestion pipelines must verify end-to-end guarantees—exactly-once semantics, idempotency, and ordering—under realistic failure conditions, not just basic endpoint validation.

## Key Concepts

- **Exactly-once delivery**: Ensuring data is processed once despite at-least-once underlying transport
- **Idempotency via batch keys**: Preventing duplicate processing when the same batch is submitted multiple times
- **Ordering guarantees**: Maintaining sequence integrity in downstream consumers
- **Fault injection**: Simulating regional outages and network partitions to verify resilience

## Code Example

```javascript
// Test harness for exactly-once verification
async function testExactlyOnce() {
  const idempotencyKey = uuidv4();
  const payload = generateTelemetryBatch(1024 * 512); // 512KB

  // Send same request multiple times (simulating retries)
  const results = await Promise.all([
    postTelemetry(payload, idempotencyKey),
    postTelemetry(payload, idempotencyKey),
    postTelemetry(payload, idempotencyKey)
  ]);

  // Verify: only ONE record in downstream store
  const downstreamCount = await countRecords(idempotencyKey);
  expect(downstreamCount).toBe(1);
}

// Test ordering under concurrent load
async function testOrdering() {
  const sequences = Array.from({length: 1000}, (_, i) => i);
  await Promise.all(sequences.map(seq => 
    postTelemetry({seq, data: `batch-${seq}`}, `key-${seq}`)
  ));

  const stored = await getAllRecords();
  const isOrdered = stored.every((r, i) => r.seq === i);
  expect(isOrdered).toBe(true);
}
```

## Test Cases

| Scenario | Tooling | Expected Outcome |
|----------|---------|------------------|
| Duplicate submits with same idempotency key | Retry harness | Single record in sink |
| Concurrent batch writes | k6/JMeter load test | All sequences preserved |
| Regional failover | Chaos mesh injection | Zero data loss, correct ordering |
| Network interruption during POST | WireMock fault simulation | Exactly-once after recovery |

## Why This Is Asked

Testing idempotent POST endpoints is critical for preventing duplicate creates and handling race conditions in production traffic. This validates basic correctness, ensures no duplicate orders under concurrent load, and confirms proper retry behavior.

## Key Concepts

- **Idempotency Keys**: Unique identifiers that allow safe retry of requests
- **Race Conditions**: Scenarios where concurrent requests may cause unintended side effects
- **HTTP Status Codes**: 201 (Created), 200 (OK), 4xx (Client Error), 5xx (Server Error), 429 (Too Many Requests)
- **Concurrency Testing**: Validating system behavior under parallel requests and retries

## Code Example

```javascript
